There's a difference in using a secure blackberry and setting up your own server / email domain. The blackberry is just a device you add an email account to, in other words, a yahoo or .gov email account. From there, the device is merely a medium to check it's contents, no different than an iPhone.

&amp;#x200B;

A server with a domain is entirely different. The server itself is configured to host it's own, private domain, ie, clinton's email which, and this is not exact, but could be set to something like @clinton.org or something. The difference is now you have emails that are not on a secured government domain and you have a system setup in which you have no idea what type of plans are in place. What type of disaster recovery is there? What type of backups are there? How often are those backups run? Most importantly, what type of security does it have? Does it have an actual firewall in place or are they just using windows server firewall or perhaps something unix / linux based?

&amp;#x200B;

The security of government may be lacking, but let's be real. There is nothing in the world that can't be hacked. The only thing you can do is try to delay the inevitable and detect when it eventually happens. With Clinton's setup, it's far different than Colin Powell using his Yahoo! or AoL account. Those are corporations with, at the very least, corporate safe guards and disaster  recovery / backup plans in place. It also looks like there is some things to hide, even if the intentions are pure. The fact that there are emails and potentially classified information that is not on the .gov domain means its existence isn't known to request via FOIA requests.

&amp;#x200B;

There are also no logs to verify that everything is potentially accounted for like in the case of those articles from where she had the server wiped via Bleach Bit. Frankly, I myself, would like to see this practice immediately halted by all parties. All official, government email should only be coming from and going to an @gov domain and no other domain. Sure they can add it to secure devices that are approved like blackberries, iPhones, etc, but the practice of using AOL, Yahoo! and Gmail should immediately be stopped. This, however is just my two cents. If I had it my way, anyone violating those protocols would immediately have their security clearance revoked by mishandling classified information. 

&amp;#x200B;

Finally, due to it's nature and how it is setup, we also have no logs available nor have any idea about what type of IPS / IDS they have setup. That's Intrusion Prevention / Detection Systems. There is a very real chance the server could be compromised and the staff not even know it. Sorry for the long response.

I definitely understand the sentiment. A secure system usually sacrifices ease of use for that security. Case in point, take passwords in a domain ie corporate setting. You can set it so the person has to change their password every 30 days, 90 days etc. Then on top of that you can set it so that password has to be at least 15 characters long with uppercase, lowercase letters, numbers AND symbols. Then you can further set it so they cant use any part of their name nor can it be one of the last 12 passwords they've used before.

&amp;#x200B;

Ideally, this password setup would be nigh impossible to crack, but your average user is going to have massive problems not only setting, but remembering a password with such explicit requirements.

&amp;#x200B;

The same is with email. When you require large passwords, verify that a user sign in with their password or so to send an email etc, those systems seem archaic and hard to use but it's usually done in the name of security. This is of course now how our government systems work I surmise, but when you are dealing with incredibly sensitive information, safeguards are taken to protect that information, often sacrificing ease of use for the user.

&amp;#x200B;

You can't have both security and ease of use. Such a system doesn't exist, unfortunately. The only thing you can do is try to find that delicate balance to where it's easy enough for users to deal with but secure enough that people can't immediately nor want to immediately try and compromise it.

&amp;#x200B;

That being said,  I don't know what Jared or Ivanka are using. However as my response above, I purely believe that all public official correspondence should come from a verified, secure, .gov domain. Anything else imo should be an immediate revocation of clearance.

