I'm a sick and twisted person, apparently. While I do agree with you, there is this dark, cynical side to me that laughs at this. I mean, it seems like it would be what sanctuary cities want. You figured they would be like, "Sure,  bus them all in!" and be welcoming and whatnot while also chastising them for abhorrent behavior.

&amp;#x200B;

Despite that, I also acknowledge that this is straight up abhorrent, if not downright incorrigible behavior. People aren't pawns and shouldn't be treated as such. Even though I vehemently disagree with sanctuary cities, this is a whole new level of low. These people should be given their due process / asylum chance if that is what they are claiming otherwise deported like usual. No point using them as pawns to make a point. Beat sanctuary cities in the courts, not by crap like this.

As Carlko said, I don't buy that this is what they want. Otherwise, cops would be able to work with immigration officials with people already apprehended. As it stands now, there are numerous laws in place that prevent officers from inquiring about immigration status. Okay, I can understand that part. However, there is also cases like [this](https://www.kgun9.com/news/local-news/ice-removed-from-pima-county-jail) or [this](https://www.ksbw.com/article/ice-agent-booted-from-monterey-county-jail/14456965). In both cases, ice officials were removed from jails as the jails no longer want to work with ICE. 

&amp;#x200B;

It's one thing to not inquire about immigration status. I think it's another when you have people being brought in on other charges (DUI, Theft, Drugs etc) in which case they should then verify the immigration status of the detainee. However, they aren't even doing that. All this is going farther than just giving them a safe haven to talk to the police. Also, see Carlko's response below.

&amp;#x200B;

Personally, I'm against sanctuary policies in any capacity. Jails and cops should totally be working with ICE. It's not their job to enforce it, but it wouldn't hurt to have an ICE agent located in jails etc to crackdown on those that are caught on other things. It irks me because they expect ICE to do the job and deport people but they don't want ICE in the jails or want to work with ICE so they know when they can pick up people for deportation.

The problems i have with this study is it usually tends to focus on violent crimes. In that case, it's true. They may not commit as many violent crimes. However, they commit a crime, albeit a misdemeanor, just on entering. In that part, the study is "factually correct." Semantically, it's incorrect.  When you take in account that they also work jobs, the majority of them fraudulently fill out the I-9 form or steal someone's SS# to do so. That's identity theft as well as falsifying government documents, both crimes.

&amp;#x200B;

To say getting rid of them would increase the crime rate is blatantly false. If we were to magically get rid of all them, that means we would prevent the crime they would have committed in the first place. There aren't going to be people that would suddenly spring up and replace them, so the crime rate wouldn't increase. That logic is backwards imo.

&amp;#x200B;

Trumps policies aren't racist. ICE is also deporting people on overstayed visas etc. If anything, I'd like to see less bluster about the wall and more crackdown on the parties exploiting them. See here and read about the [Irish man that was deported](https://www.bostonglobe.com/metro/2017/07/08/irishman-who-lived-illegally-for-decade-deported/rroAq9izMyHth0w2pj6r4O/story.html). This whole "tear down the policies and look at what's really behind it!" is not true. It's just trying to attack the argument without actually offering a solution. 

&amp;#x200B;

Policies aren't racist just because they differ from yours. This has nothing to do with keeping brown people out or any of that nonsense. It IS however attempts to deal with a system that is currently backlogged and needs to be reformed. Whether you should plug the hole first then fix the problem or fix the problem first then plug the hole is debatable.

&amp;#x200B;

Personally, if you want to deal with it, the wall isn't the best idea. Sure, it'll work. It's effectiveness may be debatable but it will work in at least some minute capacity. That being said, I'd rather they go after the employers with much,  much stricter penalties such as seizing assets / profits and even loss of business license while implementing a system like E-Verify. You crackdown on identity theft and their ability to work and the draw for coming here goes away. They will self deport after that.

There is a clear difference in local law enforcement acting as immigration agents and those laws above. The former would be like AZ trying to make officers ask immigration status. In that instance, yes, I can see them doing a federal job that is beyond their jurisdiction. In that part, you have a concise, clear argument.

&amp;#x200B;

The laws above though are meant to punish people who cooperate with ICE to a level that almost borders, if not crosses, obstruction. They removed ICE agents from jails. In that instance, law enforcement wasn't doing ICE's job. There was an ICE agent there doing ICE's job for people who came in that were detained. This doesn't make sense as that ICE agent wasn't performing police duties and arresting people that reported crime. They just alerted ICE to make pickups on those that were already detained.

&amp;#x200B;

Companies that work with ICE lose contracts in CA shows again, that it's not about the cops doing ICE's job. They are punishing anyone who even cooperates with ICE. It's clear that these sanctuary laws are not designed to encourage people to report crimes, it's straight up encouraged to protect them from deportation in almost any way they conceivably can, at least in CA. Most of the really nutty, obstructionist laws come from there. I haven't seen many other Sanctuary states take it to the level CA does.

&amp;#x200B;

As for your final comment, our departments should be working together, not infighting or trying to obstruct each other. Infighting like that leads to bad things, case in point, I believe it was the CIA / FBI I have heard mentioned could probably have prevented 9/11. However, that may very well be hearsay. Regardless, infighting between departments is always bad. Local and federal should be working together, not trying to stop each other's actions.

